<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hamani Diori</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hamani Diori was the first President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, from 1960 to 1974. A teacher and leader of the Niger Progressive Party, he was backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djibo-bakary">Djibo Bakary</a>'s Sawaba party, which was banned after the 1958 referendum. He ruled a one-party state and was a leading figure in Francophone African diplomacy until he was overthrown in a military coup by Seyni Kountché in 1974.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hamani-diori.jpg" alt="Hamani Diori" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kroon, Ron / Anefo · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hamani_Diori_1968b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
